  "body_html": "<h1>Optimal Strategy Sets</h1>\n<p>For a finite matrix game $A$, the <strong>optimal row strategy set</strong>\n$X(A) \\subseteq \\Delta(I)$ contains the mixed row strategies that achieve the\nmaximin value, i.e.\n$$\n  X(A) = \\{ x \\in \\Delta(I) : \\min_{j \\in J} E_A(x,j) = \\operatorname{val}(A) \\}.\n$$\nThe <strong>optimal column strategy set</strong> $Y(A) \\subseteq \\Delta(J)$ is defined\nsymmetrically with the maximum over pure rows equal to the value.</p>\n<p>By the minimax theorem (<a class=\"node-ref\" data-node-id=\"zero_sum.von_neumann_minimax\" href=\"zero_sum_von_neumann_minimax.html\">Von Neumann Minimax Theorem</a>) both sets are\nnonempty.</p>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[MFoGT, Prop. 2.4.1] Laraki, Renault, and Sorin, <em>Mathematical Foundations of Game Theory</em>, <code>references/MathFoundationsGameTheory.pdf</code>. Definition of X(A) and Y(A), the optimal strategy sets.</li>\n</ul>",
  "proof_html": null,
